Q: How to parse timestamp to day,month and year and time A: <h2>Whole seconds, not millis</h2>
<p>Your number <code>1443884578</code> appears to be a count of whole seconds, not milliseconds, since the epoch reference of first moment of 1970 in UTC, 1970-01-01T00:00Z.</p>
<h2>Avoid legacy date-time classes</h2>
<p>Never use the tragically-flawed legacy date-time classes such as <code>Calendar</code>, <code>Date</code>, and <code>SimpleDateFormat</code>.</p>
<h2><em>java.time</em></h2>
<p>Use only <em>java.time</em> classes for your date-time work.</p>
<p>To represent a moment as seen “in UTC” (with an offset of zero hours-minutes-seconds from the temporal meridian of UTC), use the <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs//api/java.base/java/time/Instant.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>java.time.Instant</code></a> class.</p>
<pre><code>Instant instant = Instant.ofEpochSecond( 1_443_884_578L ) ;
</code></pre>
<p>Generate text in standard ISO 8601 format.</p>
<pre><code>String output = instant.toString() ;
</code></pre>
<p>Likely you want to view that moment through the wall-clock/calendar of a particular time zone.</p>
<pre><code>ZoneId zone = ZoneId.of( &quot;America/Edmonton&quot; ) ;
ZonedDateTime zdt = instant.atZone( zone ) ;
</code></pre>
<p>Generate localized text. Use <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs//api/java.base/java/time/format/DateTimeFormatter.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>DateTimeFormatter</code></a> class.</p>
<pre><code>Locale locale = Locale.of( &quot;en&quot; , &quot;US&quot; ) ;  // In earlier Java, use `Locale.forLanguageTag( &quot;en-GB&quot; ) ;
DateTimeFormatter formatter = DateTimeFormatter.ofLocalizedDateTime( FormatStyle.FULL , FormatStyle.SHORT ).withLocale( locale ) ;
String output = zdt.format( formatter ) ;
System.out.println( output ) ;
</code></pre>
<blockquote>
<p>Saturday, October 3, 2015, 9:02 AM</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If you want to see the moment in UTC, switch above code to <code>ZoneOffset.UTC</code> as the zone.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Saturday, October 3, 2015, 3:02 PM</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Perhaps you want only the date portion. So extract a <code>LocalDate</code> object.</p>
<pre><code>LocalDate date = zdt.toLocalDate() ;
</code></pre>
<p>Generate localized text representing the content of that <code>LocalDate</code> object.</p>
<pre><code>Locale locale = Locale.of( &quot;en&quot; , &quot;GB&quot; ) ;  // In earlier Java, use `Locale.forLanguageTag( &quot;en-GB&quot; ) ;
DateTimeFormatter formatterDate = DateTimeFormatter.ofLocalizedDate( FormatStyle.MEDIUM ).withLocale( locale ) ;
String outputDate = date.format( formatterDate ) ;
</code></pre>
<blockquote>
<p>3 Oct 2015</p>
</blockquote>

Q: How to show pagination dots in owl carousel A: <p>Just change your JS to:</p>

<pre><code>$(document).ready(function(){
    $("#owl-demo").owlCarousel({
        items:1,
        loop: true,
        autoplay:true,
        autoplaySpeed: 500,
        dots:true,
        dotsContainer:'#dots',
        nav: true,
        dotsEach: true    
    });
});
</code></pre>
